 ## Architecture
 
@@ -43,7 +44,9 @@ Dev-only environment variables (see `capture.go`, `app/app.go`, `app/session.go`
 
 **Phases and routing.** `App.phase` is booting → login → ready. Once ready, `shell.go` draws the sidebar and title bar around a `ui.Router` with these routes: `/files/{path...}` (netdisk path, segments `url.PathEscape`d by `filesRoute`/`dirOfRoute`), `/search?q=`, `/transfers`, `/shares`, `/offline`, `/dedup`, `/recycle`, `/settings`. The title bar's controls switch on `a.section()`. Each directory gets its own `listing` (cached in `a.listings`, keyed by dir), so a page sliding out during a router transition keeps showing its own files.
 
-**Interface font (`internal/fontconfig`).** The interface follows this computer's fontconfig default sans-serif rather than a hard-coded face, which matters on desktops without XSettings where MyGo finds no GTK interface font. `fontconfig.Family`/`Size`/`Detected` run `fc-match -f "%{family[0]}\n%{size}\n" sans-serif` once with a 500 ms timeout and cache the answer (`sync.Once`), dropping empty and placeholder families; non-Linux builds return empty so MyGo keeps its own system font. `App.resolvedFont` turns the point size into DIP (`round(pt*4/3)`, clamped 11–17), and `App.applyTheme` sets the light/dark `ui.Theme`'s `Font`/`FontSize` from it each frame. `settings.FollowSystemFont` (nil, so true, by default) turns it off to the built-in 13 DIP and empty family; `BAIDUNETGO_FONT` and `BAIDUNETGO_FONT_SIZE` override both for tests and as an escape hatch. The settings page's 外观 section shows the detected face and a switch. A failed detection or no fontconfig is silent: the app keeps the built-in font.
+**Interface font (`internal/fontconfig`).** The interface follows this computer's fontconfig default sans-serif rather than a hard-coded face, which matters on desktops without XSettings where MyGo finds no GTK interface font. `fontconfig.List`/`Families`/`Family`/`Size`/`Detected` run `fc-match -s -f "%{family}\t%{size}\n" sans-serif` once with a 500 ms timeout and cache the answer until `Reset`. The answer is the whole ordered family chain fontconfig resolves for sans-serif — the face a fonts.conf prepends, then the fallbacks it names for glyphs that face lacks — deduplicated, without placeholders (`System`, `sans-serif`) and capped at 6, each family its ASCII name where it has one so a localized alias never stands for the family. Only the first line's size is used: fallback lines carry their own fonts' sizes (Twemoji reports 58.56). Non-Linux builds return empty so MyGo keeps its own system font. `App.resolvedFont` hands the chain, comma-separated, to `ui.Theme.Font` (MyGo's `familyList` splits it, and `pangoFamily` builds Pango's ordered fallback list) and turns the point size into DIP (`round(pt*4/3)`, clamped 11–17: MyGo lays a FontSize of 16 out the way Pango draws 12pt, as pango-view at 96dpi confirms), and `App.applyTheme` sets the light/dark `ui.Theme`'s `Font`/`FontSize` from it each frame. `settings.FollowSystemFont` (nil, so true, by default) turns it off to the built-in 13 DIP and empty family; `BAIDUNETGO_FONT` and `BAIDUNETGO_FONT_SIZE` override both for tests and as an escape hatch. The settings page's 外观 section shows the chain and the points/DIP in effect, with a 重新检测 button that calls `fontconfig.Reset` and the follow switch. A failed detection or no fontconfig is silent: the app keeps the built-in font.
+
+**Profiles and XDG.** A build that needs its own saved session, settings and transfers (the patched build beside the installed app) gets them from `BAIDUNETGO_PROFILE`, which `main.go`'s `useProfile` turns into `mygo.App.SetPath(PathUserData/PathCache, …)` before `App.Run`; `tools/install-patched-local.sh`'s launcher sets it and changes no XDG variable. Never give a profile its own configuration by pointing `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` elsewhere: fontconfig reads its fonts.conf there, and GTK and dconf their settings, so a patched build launched that way lost the user's fonts and drew fontconfig's Droid Sans fallback — which is exactly why the font looked like it could not be applied.
 
 **Overlays.** One `a.dlg` (`dialog` with a `dialogKind`) drives every modal through `a.dialogs`. The file preview is a separate overlay (`a.pv`, `previewOverlay`). The image preview and the grid/list thumbnails share `ui.ElementTransition{Match: thumbMatch{fsid}}`. The source thumbnail is skipped while `a.previewing(f)`, so the image grows out of it and shrinks back.
 
